What are the five classes of this file
--------------------------------------

This file contains five classes:

 * CMGBitBoard is a class to manipulate bitboards (see next section).
 * CMGPosition is a class that regroups useful position-related functions, such as conversions to and from a string, move generation, etc.
 * CMGMove is a class that regroups useful move-related functions, such as conversions to and from a string, validation, etc.
 * CMGPiece is a class that regroups useful piece-related functions
 * CMGUtil is a class with some general-purpose functions

What are bitBoards
------------------

Bitboards are used to represent specific aspects of a chess position. Each square of the board is represented by a bit of a 64-bit value.
This is bigger than the [maximal exact-precision integer defined by ECMAScript](http://ecma262-5.com/ELS5_HTML.htm#Section_8.5).
For this reason, I have decided to represent each bitboard with an array of four integers. More information about this can be found in the file bitbard-generator.coffee.

###

class CMGBitBoard

    # Note:
    # @SQUARE_VALUES and @SQUARE_QUADRANTS_AND_VALUES are generated by a metaprogram and placed at the END of this javascript

    # -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    # Bitboard maths (functions of class and of instance)

    @clone: (bb) ->
        ###
        Clone the specified bitBoard
        @param bb (bitboard)
        @return bitboard
        ###
        [bb[0], bb[1], bb[2], bb[3]]

    @binEqual: (bb1, bb2) ->
        ###
        Are the two specified bitboards equal
        @param bb1 (bitboard)
        @param bb2 (bitboard)
        @return boolean
        ###
        for quadrantId in [0..3]
            if bb1[quadrantId] isnt bb2[quadrantId]
                return false
        return true

    @boolNand: (bb1, bb2) ->
        ###
        NAND operation on two bitboard

        On average, this function should be quicker than "@isZero( @binAnd(bb1, bb2) )".

        @param bb1 (bitboard)
        @param bb2 (bitboard)
        @return boolean
        ###
        (0 is (bb1[0] & bb2[0])) and (0 is (bb1[1] & bb2[1])) and (0 is (bb1[2] & bb2[2])) and (0 is (bb1[3] & bb2[3]))

    @binAnd: (bb1, bb2) ->
        ###
        Binary AND operation
        @param bb1 (bitboard)
        @param bb2 (bitboard)
        @return bitboard
        ###
        [
            bb1[0] & bb2[0],
            bb1[1] & bb2[1],
            bb1[2] & bb2[2],
            bb1[3] & bb2[3]
        ]

    @binOr: (bb1, bb2) ->
        ###
        Binary OR operation
        @param bb1 (bitboard)
        @param bb2 (bitboard)
        @return bitboard
        ###
        [
            bb1[0] | bb2[0],
            bb1[1] | bb2[1],
            bb1[2] | bb2[2],
            bb1[3] | bb2[3]
        ]

    @binNot: (bb) ->
        ###
        Binary NOT operation
        @param bb (bitboard)
        @return bitboard
        ###
        [
            ~ bb[0] & 0xffff,
            ~ bb[1] & 0xffff,
            ~ bb[2] & 0xffff,
            ~ bb[3] & 0xffff
        ]

    @valueOfSquare: (square) ->
        ###
        Bitboard corresponding to the specified square
        @param square (integer)
        @return bitboard
        ###
        @SQUARE_VALUES[square]

    @quadrantAndValueOfSquare: (square) ->
        ###
        Bitboard quadrant and value in quadrant corresponding to the specified square
        @param square (integer)
        @return array(integer, integer)
        ###
        @SQUARE_QUADRANTS_AND_VALUES[square]

    @bitBoardToSquareKeyArray: ( bitBoard ) ->
        ###
        Get an array of all square keys where the bits of the bitboard equal 1
        @param bitBoard (bitboard)
        @return array
        ###
        squareKeys = []
        for quadrant, qid in bitBoard
            for key in [0..15]
                if (1 << key) & quadrant
                    squareKeys.push(qid * 16 + key)
        return squareKeys

